1
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:11,310
Then why did you become a teacher?

2
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:15,580
Isn't it because you want to become
your version of an ideal teacher?

3
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:17,350
I suppose so.

4
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:22,890
But if I only followed my ideals,
I'd never get anywhere.

5
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:27,310
That's why I choose to accomplish
the bare minimum every day.

6
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:29,500
The bare minimum?

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,570
To protect the lives of my students.

8
00:00:48,410 --> 00:00:51,760
You're a cool guy.

9
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:54,640
Huh?

10
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:57,300
You're cool.

11
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:02,490
<i>Cool?</i>

12
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:07,870
<i>N-No, she just...</i>

13
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:10,200
<i>She wouldn't get it.</i>

14
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:12,720
Saya.

15
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:17,950
Saya, clean up the table.

16
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:21,580
Okay, I'm almost done.

17
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:27,500
<i>How could a person from Edo understand</i>

18
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,020
<i>the struggle of living in this modern era?</i>

19
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:31,970
Enough gaming.

20
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:35,150
Set the chopsticks and cups.

21
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:36,800
Fine.

22
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:39,480
Where's Ms. Nashiki?

23
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,750
Your room.

24
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:43,750
- What?
- She wanted to borrow a dictionary.

25
00:01:45,210 --> 00:01:46,850
Seriously?

26
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:49,550
Ms. Nashiki, please don't barge...

27
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:54,870
Ikuru.

28
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:58,650
Is this all true?

29
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:05,740
After the 15th Tokugawa shogun, their rule...

30
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:07,620
It ends?

31
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:10,480
Yeah.

32
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:16,070
But Lord Tsunayoshi was just appointed
the 5th shogun.

33
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:19,600
Tsunayoshi is especially famous
among the Tokugawa's.

34
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:21,980
Why? Did something happen during his rule?

35
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:23,400
No, nothing bad.

36
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:25,150
Let's see... Found it.

37
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:28,680
He enacted an effective animal welfare law.

38
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:33,430
It punished those who brought harm
onto animals, specifically dogs.

39
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:35,580
Which is why he was called, "The Dog Shogun".

40
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:37,200
The Dog Shogun?

41
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,110
What a nasty insult!

42
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,180
I see now why he's infamous.

43
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:49,350
But my guys would've liked him.
We're all dog lovers, after all.

44
00:02:50,250 --> 00:02:52,310
<i>We had a stray that visited often.</i>

45
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:54,720
<i>We called the little lad, "Mame".</i>

46
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:56,630
Mame!

47
00:02:56,630 --> 00:02:58,890
Nice to see you, boy!

48
00:02:58,890 --> 00:03:01,320
Come here!

49
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:02,380
Good boy!

50
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:04,250
How are you?

51
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:07,950
Recently, Tsunayoshi's reputation
has been re-evaluated.

52
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:11,850
People claim his welfare laws
were meant to protect all lives.

53
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:16,300
Like modern day general welfare,
it was for all who were less fortunate.

54
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:21,000
What's most amazing though,
is that this was enacted so long ago.

55
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:26,890
He lived in an era of turmoil,
where lives were less valued.

56
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:30,600
Yet he emphasized the meaning of living,
and one's life.

57
00:03:31,190 --> 00:03:33,500
I'm sure he must have had a wise advisor.

58
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:41,200
There are plenty of intelligent folks
like Lord Tsunayoshi.

59
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,020
<i>Like a mathematical genius
who directly served the daimyo.</i>

60
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,030
Now he was renowned throughout Japan.

61
00:03:47,030 --> 00:03:51,030
A mathematician known all across Japan...
Like Takakazu Seki?

62
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:53,430
Wait... you know Master Seki?

63
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:54,840
Huh? Yes.

64
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:57,080
- What?
- Hold on.

65
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:58,410
[History of Mathematicians in Japan]

66
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,660
"Takakazu Seki, Lord of Mathematics".

67
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:07,900
Lord of Mathematics...

68
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,140
Then he is being lauded as an expert?

69
00:04:14,330 --> 00:04:16,610
What a perfect title!

70
00:04:16,610 --> 00:04:19,960
So this is how Master Seki
is being remembered.

71
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:21,590
You know him?

72
00:04:21,590 --> 00:04:24,590
Yeah. He lived nearby.

73
00:04:24,590 --> 00:04:27,510
In my heart, I call him my teacher.

74
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:28,570
He taught you?

75
00:04:28,570 --> 00:04:33,180
Of course not. He was a warrior,
and I, the daughter of a commoner.

76
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:38,890
But he is a humble and kind man.

77
00:04:38,890 --> 00:04:40,240
And...

78
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:42,960
Mame! You're such a doll.

79
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:47,270
So you call that dog, "Mame".

80
00:04:47,270 --> 00:04:48,680
Master Seki!

81
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,080
You know of this pup?

82
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,340
Indeed. He often visits my place as well.

83
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:54,550
Though I call him, "Ritsu".
(T/N: "Ratio")

84
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:57,340
Ritsu? What an odd name.

85
00:04:57,340 --> 00:04:59,440
Sounds like a mathematical problem.

86
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:02,310
I suppose so.

87
00:05:02,310 --> 00:05:06,950
I cannot help but think of ratios all day,
and all night.

88
00:05:06,950 --> 00:05:10,780
Still, that is no reason
to forget your meals.

89
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:15,010
Ritsu, make sure to have Master Seki
feed you well, alright?

90
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:20,260
It seems I've met my match.

91
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,040
Ritsu.

92
00:05:25,890 --> 00:05:27,670
How adorable.

93
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:32,930
Ritsu, of course. What a doll!

94
00:05:33,510 --> 00:05:35,280
Ritsu.

95
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:36,950
Are you hot?

96
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:50,050
What're you two doing? I'm starving.

97
00:05:50,050 --> 00:05:52,150
We'll be right there.

98
00:05:52,150 --> 00:05:53,760
Sorry about that.

99
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:00,100
<i>What was all that about?</i>

100
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:06,430
<i>That look on her face...</i>

101
00:06:06,430 --> 00:06:08,190
This thing is so fluffy!

102
00:06:08,190 --> 00:06:10,450
But it looks like a steamed bun.

103
00:06:10,450 --> 00:06:13,160
That's called a shumai. It's good, right?

104
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:16,050
<i>She wasn't thinking of an acquaintance.</i>

105
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:18,530
How's this thing made?

106
00:06:18,530 --> 00:06:20,640
Watch a video, I'm sure it's easy.

107
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:21,880
A video?

108
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:26,000
<i>She definitely harbored feelings for him.</i>

109
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,030
<i>Ms. Nashiki had a crush on Takakazu Seki?</i>

110
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:34,820
Ikuru. Ikuru!

111
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:37,440
What are you doing?

112
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:40,630
My shumai...

113
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:42,780
I'll go wash it off.

114
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:45,760
Where's your mind at?

115
00:06:47,030 --> 00:06:49,260
All that sodium, it'd kill him.

116
00:07:05,770 --> 00:07:07,290
Woah!

117
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:09,600
They finished cooking in an instant.

118
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:12,220
Only because they edited the video.

119
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,410
"Edited"?

120
00:07:13,410 --> 00:07:16,560
Anyway, they're telling you
it's practically this easy.

121
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:18,920
I see.

122
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:23,470
This thing called a "smartphone"
is quite the contraption.

123
00:07:23,470 --> 00:07:25,490
Yeah. You can look up anything.

124
00:07:25,490 --> 00:07:29,920
All thanks to the World Wide Web.
[Rinka Saeki]

125
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,940
<i>Ikuru.</i>
[Rinka Saeki]

126
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:34,660
You better come today.

127
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:36,740
I'll be waiting until you arrive.

128
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:40,110
- Ikuru.
- Huh?

129
00:07:40,110 --> 00:07:42,820
Does that mean we won't be needed one day?

130
00:07:44,090 --> 00:07:49,010
Like couriers,
will teachers become unnecessary, too?

131
00:07:50,830 --> 00:07:53,200
- Of course not.
- Really?

132
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:56,410
Less people want to become teachers.
It's possible.

133
00:07:56,410 --> 00:08:00,310
Dummy. Teachers teach more
than just textbook knowledge.

134
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:05,800
Same goes for Edo period teachers, right?

135
00:08:09,370 --> 00:08:11,450
You're right.

136
00:08:20,910 --> 00:08:23,990
[Episode 3]

137
00:08:23,990 --> 00:08:27,800
[Narita Airport Terminal 1]

138
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:45,400
<i>Pathetic as I may be,
I have been told I was liked once.</i>

139
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:49,470
And what year was he appointed? Saeki.

140
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:53,400
Hey. Saeki.

141
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,220
Rinka. Rinka, wake up!

142
00:08:56,220 --> 00:08:59,610
Saeki. Wake up!

143
00:09:02,150 --> 00:09:05,300
Is my class that boring to you?

144
00:09:07,590 --> 00:09:09,140
Out in the hall!

145
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:13,670
What?

146
00:09:18,710 --> 00:09:20,850
Sir.

147
00:09:20,850 --> 00:09:22,180
What?

148
00:09:23,290 --> 00:09:26,040
You should file a divorce already.

149
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:28,590
I think your relationship
is far beyond repair.

150
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,530
Settle down!

151
00:09:37,570 --> 00:09:40,430
Rinka, class is over.

152
00:09:53,250 --> 00:09:55,510
Damn it...

153
00:09:57,170 --> 00:09:59,140
I'm so unlucky.

154
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:01,000
No.

155
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,950
You're super lucky.

156
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:07,140
You shouldn't mess with
our teacher like that.

157
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,800
Why not?

158
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:10,890
I was only telling him the truth.

159
00:10:10,890 --> 00:10:13,220
The truth?

160
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:16,880
<i>His shirt collar was a bit dirty.</i>

161
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:20,240
<i>I bet his wife isn't doing laundry for him.</i>

162
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:22,640
And I saw he hired a private detective.

163
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,170
<i>That already proves
their relationship is over.</i>

164
00:10:25,170 --> 00:10:26,910
Who are you, Sherlock Holmes?

165
00:10:26,910 --> 00:10:29,530
You saw how angry he was.

166
00:10:29,530 --> 00:10:31,960
I'd never want to marry a guy like him.

167
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:35,860
That's just your opinion.

168
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:37,900
Fair enough.

169
00:10:40,070 --> 00:10:42,680
Keep this up, and you'll never make friends.

170
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:44,490
I don't need friends.

171
00:10:44,490 --> 00:10:47,000
And it's not like anyone's bullying me.

172
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,410
Besides, observing people
is exhausting enough.

173
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:55,700
As your parent, I worry about you.

174
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:57,150
- My parent?
- Yeah.

175
00:10:57,150 --> 00:10:59,010
You?

176
00:10:59,010 --> 00:11:02,640
You grew attached to me
like a baby bird, remember?

177
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:04,740
Wait.

178
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,200
Let's go home.

179
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:25,180
Okay!

180
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,960
What, you think I'm a baby chick?

181
00:11:31,700 --> 00:11:34,540
Well, I won't deny it.

182
00:11:39,870 --> 00:11:42,330
- I like you.
- Huh?

183
00:11:42,330 --> 00:11:45,740
Your unluckiness, that is.

184
00:11:46,230 --> 00:11:49,580
You never blame others
for something bad happening,

185
00:11:49,580 --> 00:11:51,540
nor hate them for it.

186
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:55,030
And you have this refreshing,
clear aura about you.

187
00:11:55,030 --> 00:11:58,600
You're not making any sense.

188
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:00,620
What?

189
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:03,210
I'm taking a nap. Night!

190
00:12:08,350 --> 00:12:10,040
Rinka!

191
00:12:16,710 --> 00:12:18,320
You're late.

192
00:12:19,110 --> 00:12:20,350
Don't call me out here without warning.

193
00:12:20,350 --> 00:12:24,290
Why not? You don't have anyone else anyway.

194
00:12:24,290 --> 00:12:25,680
Huh?

195
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:31,100
I have Saya, and my job.

196
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:35,580
So, today's the day, right? We gotta do it.

197
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:38,950
Okay, here goes. Come here.

198
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:45,810
What time is your flight?

199
00:12:45,810 --> 00:12:48,290
- Are you going to Europe again?
- China.

200
00:12:48,290 --> 00:12:50,960
I'll be competing at the WGOP.

201
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:52,000
What's that?

202
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,160
The World Grand Prix of Poker.

203
00:12:56,160 --> 00:13:00,650
Win this, and I'll be the poker world champ.

204
00:13:00,650 --> 00:13:02,390
How much is the prize pool?

205
00:13:02,390 --> 00:13:05,560
- Several hundreds of millions.
- Woah.

206
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:08,710
Then go become the champ already.

207
00:13:11,410 --> 00:13:12,850
Come here.

208
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:20,610
Hurry up, you're embarrasing me.

209
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:22,680
Bring it in.

210
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:26,010
Is this what you want?

211
00:13:34,130 --> 00:13:35,750
Hurry up.

212
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:48,450
Are you sure this will help?

213
00:13:50,130 --> 00:13:51,560
Yup.

214
00:13:53,200 --> 00:13:54,560
Thanks.

215
00:13:54,560 --> 00:13:55,950
Sure.

216
00:13:56,940 --> 00:13:58,450
See you.

217
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:02,920
Good luck.

218
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:04,420
Yeah.

219
00:14:07,110 --> 00:14:08,240
Bet.

220
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:09,230
All in.

221
00:14:09,230 --> 00:14:10,690
All in.

222
00:14:13,070 --> 00:14:14,250
Call.

223
00:14:14,250 --> 00:14:15,550
All in, called.

224
00:14:15,550 --> 00:14:18,010
King.

225
00:14:20,890 --> 00:14:24,000
If anyone found out, they'd die of laughter.

226
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,030
To think this is your good luck routine.

227
00:14:29,910 --> 00:14:31,760
Actually, never mind.

228
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:33,110
Huh?

229
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:39,510
You have someone else, don't you?

230
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:43,070
- Huh?
- Your heart rate increased.

231
00:14:43,070 --> 00:14:45,580
N-No? No, no, no!

232
00:14:45,580 --> 00:14:47,400
I knew it.

233
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:51,390
There's someone else you're interested in.

234
00:14:52,350 --> 00:14:56,260
I thought I was the only one
who had you at their whims.

235
00:14:56,260 --> 00:14:58,000
But I guess that's not true anymore?

236
00:14:58,000 --> 00:14:59,680
What are you talking about?

237
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:02,010
This is an important competition, right?

238
00:15:02,010 --> 00:15:03,100
Come on, bring it in.

239
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:06,810
You don't need to act like my parent anymore.

240
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:13,040
We've known each other for a long time now,

241
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:15,780
but our routine was the oddity.

242
00:15:16,590 --> 00:15:20,740
We got too comfortable
with our relationship, didn't we?

243
00:15:22,070 --> 00:15:24,030
Well...

244
00:15:24,930 --> 00:15:26,690
I guess so.

245
00:15:27,670 --> 00:15:32,050
We got too comfortable with our routine.

246
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:38,680
We're only childhood friends,
yet I thought I was so special.

247
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:40,310
I'm...

248
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:44,150
I'm sorry.

249
00:15:45,490 --> 00:15:48,010
And if I said I'd forgive you?

250
00:15:48,550 --> 00:15:51,960
I'll forgive you, Ikuru.

251
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,460
If you're honest with me.

252
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:56,710
Honest?

253
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:03,200
Who's on your mind right now?

254
00:16:33,510 --> 00:16:35,980
Your heart rate's gotten even quicker.

255
00:16:39,440 --> 00:16:41,660
Of course it would.

256
00:16:41,660 --> 00:16:44,000
Then I've successfully overwritten them.

257
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:49,210
I'll win and be right back,
so wait for me, okay?

258
00:16:56,450 --> 00:16:59,200
<i>I thought that was going to be my first!</i>

259
00:17:00,110 --> 00:17:01,650
<i>What's going on?</i>

260
00:17:01,650 --> 00:17:03,130
<i>What was all that about?</i>

261
00:17:03,130 --> 00:17:06,360
<i>Was she teasing me? Or being serious?</i>

262
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:10,800
<i>Am I finally graduating
from childhood friend,</i>

263
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:14,340
<i>to boyfriend status?</i>

264
00:17:31,850 --> 00:17:35,180
You scared me.

265
00:17:35,180 --> 00:17:36,410
Welcome back.

266
00:17:36,410 --> 00:17:38,980
I have returned home.

267
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:41,940
<i>Why do I feel guilty?</i>

268
00:17:41,940 --> 00:17:44,140
Your hair is wet. Use the hairdryer.

269
00:17:44,140 --> 00:17:46,740
What a pain. I'll let it air dry.

270
00:17:46,740 --> 00:17:48,680
If you don't blow dry it,
you'll wake up with cowlicks,

271
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:50,360
and create more trouble for yourself.

272
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:54,500
Those little troubles,
build up into a huge pain.

273
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:55,820
So here.

274
00:17:55,820 --> 00:17:56,780
What're you talking about?

275
00:17:56,780 --> 00:17:59,870
I'm speaking common sense here.

276
00:17:59,870 --> 00:18:02,100
Like a nagging sister-in-law.

277
00:18:02,100 --> 00:18:03,780
Night.

278
00:18:03,780 --> 00:18:05,400
What?

279
00:18:06,740 --> 00:18:10,360
<i>Who's on your mind right now?</i>

280
00:18:11,070 --> 00:18:14,570
<i>That's actually a great question.</i>

281
00:18:15,070 --> 00:18:18,800
<i>Who is on my mind right now?</i>

282
00:18:20,750 --> 00:18:24,430
<i>Who... Or rather, which one?</i>

283
00:18:26,420 --> 00:18:29,570
No, what do you mean, which one?

284
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:33,840
Why are you so spooked?

285
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:35,810
I changed my mind.

286
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:43,680
[Teacher's Office]

287
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:46,700
- Morning.
- I'm so sleepy.

288
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:48,720
How many days until summer vacation?

289
00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:51,040
One, two... Agh, the Sports Festival.

290
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:54,000
'Tis the season, again.

291
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,060
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

292
00:18:56,060 --> 00:18:59,000
Tea is served, folks.

293
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,340
Hey, what are you doing?

294
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:02,650
I don't know whose cup is whose...

295
00:19:02,650 --> 00:19:06,070
You brewed us tea, Ms. Nashiki?

296
00:19:06,070 --> 00:19:07,200
Yes, sir.

297
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:10,090
The electric kettle's an amazing invention.

298
00:19:10,090 --> 00:19:14,040
The water boils without any fire! Right?

299
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:15,980
- Right...
- Ms. Nashiki.

300
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:18,850
Are you doing this out of obligation?

301
00:19:18,850 --> 00:19:22,030
- Obligation?
- Are you all thirsty?

302
00:19:22,030 --> 00:19:24,030
- Miss! Thanks a million.
- Thank you.

303
00:19:24,030 --> 00:19:25,560
- Careful, it's hot.
- Thanks.

304
00:19:25,560 --> 00:19:27,500
Don't burn yourselves.

305
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:31,540
Tea's ready.

306
00:19:31,540 --> 00:19:34,960
Come get some. Careful!

307
00:19:35,660 --> 00:19:37,190
No obligation at all.

308
00:19:37,190 --> 00:19:38,800
Now here, have some.

309
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,810
- Thank you.
- Sure.

310
00:19:40,810 --> 00:19:42,620
- I appreciate it.
- No problem.

311
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:43,810
Ms. Akutsu, here.

312
00:19:43,810 --> 00:19:47,080
Excuse me. Here you go.

313
00:19:48,430 --> 00:19:51,350
Come and get your tea!

314
00:19:51,350 --> 00:19:53,020
Tea is great on Monday mornings.

315
00:19:53,020 --> 00:19:55,070
- Agreed.
- Thank you.

316
00:19:55,070 --> 00:19:56,640
Of course.

317
00:19:56,640 --> 00:19:58,160
- Ms. Nashiki.
- Yes?

318
00:19:58,160 --> 00:20:01,280
You have my cup there.

319
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:03,140
- Which one?
- That one there.

320
00:20:03,140 --> 00:20:05,660
Ah, this one! Here you go.

321
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:07,750
Oh my.

322
00:20:07,750 --> 00:20:11,590
My tea leaf is upright. Look here.

323
00:20:11,590 --> 00:20:14,180
- What a great sign!
- It's a first for me.

324
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:15,380
Today's your lucky day.

325
00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:17,360
Really? Goodness.

326
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:19,860
It must be because you brewed it.

327
00:20:19,860 --> 00:20:22,420
- You think so?
- Thank you.

328
00:20:22,420 --> 00:20:24,520
Of course.

329
00:20:24,520 --> 00:20:28,030
Who else... Is this yours?

330
00:20:28,030 --> 00:20:30,070
- Yes, thank you.
- Sure thing.

331
00:20:30,070 --> 00:20:34,060
Who's left? This is a fine cup.

332
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:35,330
Huh?

333
00:20:35,330 --> 00:20:36,380
That one would be mine.

334
00:20:36,380 --> 00:20:38,460
Ms. Principal!

335
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:41,300
- You have excellent taste.
- My!

336
00:20:41,300 --> 00:20:42,550
- Thank you.
- I shall carry it in.

337
00:20:42,550 --> 00:20:45,820
- Why, thank you.
- Of course.

338
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:50,070
Do I have to do that too?

339
00:20:50,070 --> 00:20:51,990
No.

340
00:20:58,210 --> 00:21:00,960
Damn it.

341
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:03,140
Why did she brew them tea?

342
00:21:03,140 --> 00:21:07,990
She's going to make tea serving
a job for us women, again.

343
00:21:07,990 --> 00:21:11,330
The rights we fought for
will go down the drain!

344
00:21:11,330 --> 00:21:14,200
It's just tea. Get it yourselves, you know?

345
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:15,330
Exactly.

346
00:21:15,330 --> 00:21:20,340
- Those men when they get tea...
- They look so full of themselves.

347
00:21:20,340 --> 00:21:23,280
What's so wrong about serving tea?

348
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:26,970
I didn't see anything about that
on the manual...

349
00:21:32,120 --> 00:21:34,830
[Become a proper teacher at my own pace]

350
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:45,580
[Math]

351
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:10,440
Everyone ready for the science quiz?

352
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:12,860
Yes, Mr. Majima.

353
00:22:12,860 --> 00:22:15,190
Then please log in to your tablets.

354
00:22:15,190 --> 00:22:17,110
Okay.

355
00:22:18,330 --> 00:22:20,900
- I've logged in.
- Me too.

356
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:22,720
Everyone ready?

357
00:22:22,720 --> 00:22:24,540
Mr. Majima.

358
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:27,270
Naoya's not logged in yet.

359
00:22:29,850 --> 00:22:31,960
Everyone else, start on your quizzes.

360
00:22:31,960 --> 00:22:33,830
I'll set the timer to 10 minutes.

361
00:22:33,830 --> 00:22:36,360
Ready? Begin.

362
00:22:41,570 --> 00:22:44,390
Sano, you need to learn
how to navigate the tablet.

363
00:22:44,390 --> 00:22:47,310
- Yes, Mr. Majima.
- Remember, first you click here to login.

364
00:22:47,310 --> 00:22:49,250
Do you know your ID?

365
00:22:49,250 --> 00:22:51,630
- No.
- Okay.

366
00:22:51,630 --> 00:22:53,690
Let's see...

367
00:22:53,690 --> 00:22:58,670
Points will be docked off
for incorrect strokes.

368
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:01,530
Remember, I can see your overall percentile,

369
00:23:01,530 --> 00:23:04,300
and time it took for you all to answer.

370
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:07,200
And one more reminder.

371
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:11,850
I can see your search histories as well.

372
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:16,140
I will repeat.

373
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:20,220
I am able to see your search history.

374
00:23:20,220 --> 00:23:22,990
Alright, settle down.

375
00:23:26,150 --> 00:23:30,070
It's time for mathematics... I mean, math.

376
00:23:30,070 --> 00:23:31,520
Yes, Ms. Nashiki.

377
00:23:31,520 --> 00:23:33,600
Get seated.

378
00:23:34,230 --> 00:23:38,660
Oh, uh...
we don't need those tablet thingies.

379
00:23:38,660 --> 00:23:40,610
What?

380
00:23:40,610 --> 00:23:43,480
We won't be using our tablets?

381
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:48,170
Nope. Today, we'll be getting to know
this lad here.

382
00:23:48,780 --> 00:23:50,030
An oval!

383
00:23:50,030 --> 00:23:53,480
No, that's a circle.

384
00:23:53,480 --> 00:23:55,590
Good answer.

385
00:23:55,590 --> 00:23:57,480
An oval...

386
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:02,920
Could be drawn like this, or this,
and still be an oval.

387
00:24:03,770 --> 00:24:08,240
But in mathematics... math,

388
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:15,240
a circle is made up of points,
that are from a given center.

389
00:24:16,020 --> 00:24:17,920
The center.

390
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:19,650
The radius.

391
00:24:19,650 --> 00:24:21,900
The diameter.

392
00:24:21,900 --> 00:24:23,500
The circumference.

393
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:26,960
Many things make up a circle.

394
00:24:26,960 --> 00:24:30,170
Today, we will be getting to know
what makes a circle.

395
00:24:30,170 --> 00:24:31,470
Yes, Ms. Nashiki.

396
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:35,480
First, let's try to find
the area of a circle.

397
00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:37,360
What?

398
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:41,160
You're free to cut, fold,
or do whatever with the circle.

399
00:24:41,160 --> 00:24:43,490
Let's brainstorm how to find this area.

400
00:24:43,490 --> 00:24:46,360
- I'm allowed to cut it?
- Of course.

401
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:49,570
Ms. Nashiki, let's just use our tablets.

402
00:24:49,570 --> 00:24:51,370
I already learned this in cram school.

403
00:24:51,370 --> 00:24:53,550
So did I.

404
00:24:53,550 --> 00:24:56,030
See, look at my tablet.

405
00:24:56,030 --> 00:25:01,200
The area of a circle is found by multiplying
the radius with itself, and 3.14.

406
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:08,400
The ratio of a circle's circumference
to its diameter, is 3.14!

407
00:25:22,270 --> 00:25:26,200
Master Seki,
is this the "ratio" you spoke of?

408
00:25:26,200 --> 00:25:30,580
Indeed. I was searching for a way
to accurately measure the area of a circle.

409
00:25:30,580 --> 00:25:37,480
I started by hypothesizing a polygon
that would be closest to a circle.

410
00:25:37,480 --> 00:25:39,710
A polygon, close to a circle.

411
00:25:39,710 --> 00:25:44,250
In theory, the area of this polygon
would also give me the area of the circle.

412
00:25:44,250 --> 00:25:45,630
I see.

413
00:25:45,630 --> 00:25:49,370
How many sides of this hypothetical polygon
have you calculated thus far?

414
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:55,820
I've currently calculated 131,072 sides.

415
00:25:55,820 --> 00:26:01,000
131,072 sides?

416
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,640
This calculation has brought me
to a certain equation.

417
00:26:06,210 --> 00:26:08,990
If you multiply the diameter of a circle
to a certain number,

418
00:26:08,990 --> 00:26:10,380
you get its area.

419
00:26:10,380 --> 00:26:12,440
Such a number exists?

420
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:16,250
I've calculated up to 11 digits
of this number thus far.

421
00:26:16,250 --> 00:26:21,650
The number being, 3.14159265359.

422
00:26:21,650 --> 00:26:25,670
Huh? What? 3.1415...

423
00:26:25,670 --> 00:26:30,070
If you only want an approximation
of the area, 3.14 will do.

424
00:26:30,700 --> 00:26:36,890
I have decided to call this number,
the circumference ratio.

425
00:26:36,890 --> 00:26:39,440
The circumference ratio...

426
00:26:40,450 --> 00:26:43,510
3.14...

427
00:26:44,640 --> 00:26:46,850
I know about 3.14, too.

428
00:26:46,850 --> 00:26:49,000
- So do I!
- Me too!

429
00:27:09,720 --> 00:27:12,060
Ms. Nashiki, what's wrong?

430
00:27:12,060 --> 00:27:15,350
Do you not know about 3.14?

431
00:27:15,350 --> 00:27:17,330
Ms. Nashiki?

432
00:27:17,330 --> 00:27:19,840
No, I do.

433
00:27:25,700 --> 00:27:31,380
This number is very important to me.

434
00:27:31,900 --> 00:27:37,160
It was a number discovered
through perseverance and effort

435
00:27:37,160 --> 00:27:39,970
by my very own teacher.

436
00:27:40,570 --> 00:27:47,140
That's why I want you all to puzzle out
how to find the area, alright?

437
00:27:47,140 --> 00:27:49,150
Yes, Ms. Nashiki!

438
00:27:57,240 --> 00:27:59,980
My dad bought me a Switch 2!

439
00:27:59,980 --> 00:28:01,630
- What?
- Lucky!

440
00:28:01,630 --> 00:28:02,840
Awesome, right?

441
00:28:02,840 --> 00:28:05,440
Does anyone know how to
use commands in Minecraft?

442
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:08,230
I do! I can spawn items even in a desert.

443
00:28:08,230 --> 00:28:10,320
- Seriously?
- That's sick!

444
00:28:10,320 --> 00:28:11,540
I made a friend on Roblox, too.

445
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:13,180
So who's coming over today?

446
00:28:13,180 --> 00:28:16,310
- Me!
- Me too!

447
00:28:16,310 --> 00:28:18,160
You coming too, Naoya?

448
00:28:19,860 --> 00:28:21,330
Of course you aren't!

449
00:28:21,330 --> 00:28:25,410
You can't login, or even play Mario Kart!

450
00:28:26,570 --> 00:28:30,250
Come on, let's go!

451
00:28:45,440 --> 00:28:48,110
That looks great!

452
00:28:48,110 --> 00:28:50,220
You got an interesting pattern there.

453
00:28:50,790 --> 00:28:52,000
I see you're making it look cool.

454
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:53,550
I'm gonna try attaching this to it.

455
00:28:53,550 --> 00:28:56,630
Sounds good.

456
00:28:56,630 --> 00:28:58,430
Ms. Nashiki, look! Flowers!

457
00:28:58,430 --> 00:29:00,630
- They're looking real pretty.
- It's cute!

458
00:29:00,630 --> 00:29:02,120
Right?

459
00:29:02,120 --> 00:29:05,610
Ms. Nashiki, look!

460
00:29:05,610 --> 00:29:07,040
Yeah, that's a big one.

461
00:29:07,040 --> 00:29:10,600
Ms. Nashiki, look at mine!

462
00:29:10,600 --> 00:29:13,340
What're you guys making?

463
00:29:13,340 --> 00:29:16,740
I see. Looks interesting.

464
00:29:16,740 --> 00:29:19,110
Ms. Nashiki. Ryota's messing around.

465
00:29:19,110 --> 00:29:20,310
He cut his all up.

466
00:29:20,310 --> 00:29:23,000
I'm just making pizza! See?

467
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:25,460
This side is mayo-corn,
and this side is teriyaki.

468
00:29:25,460 --> 00:29:26,980
Pizza? I want a slice!

469
00:29:26,980 --> 00:29:28,320
Pizza?

470
00:29:28,320 --> 00:29:31,900
I cut a slice for everyone.

471
00:29:33,460 --> 00:29:36,300
Ms. Nashiki, this is a waste of time.

472
00:29:36,300 --> 00:29:40,440
I want to do a normal class, not this.

473
00:29:40,440 --> 00:29:44,510
But Minato, Yuzuki. Look.

474
00:29:44,510 --> 00:29:48,200
Ryota just discovered
the true nature of a circle.

475
00:29:48,200 --> 00:29:50,470
Huh? I did?

476
00:29:50,470 --> 00:29:53,300
This is what makes circles so interesting.

477
00:29:53,300 --> 00:29:55,190
Watch.

478
00:29:55,190 --> 00:30:01,180
If you take this "pizza",
and line up the pieces

479
00:30:01,180 --> 00:30:03,190
like so...

480
00:30:03,190 --> 00:30:06,910
Keep watching.

481
00:30:08,010 --> 00:30:09,740
What's it making?

482
00:30:11,080 --> 00:30:13,500
I'll keep stacking.

483
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:15,140
See? What's that?

484
00:30:15,140 --> 00:30:17,760
Hey, it became a long rectangle.

485
00:30:17,760 --> 00:30:20,710
- You're right.
- A rectangle!

486
00:30:20,710 --> 00:30:22,200
Does that mean...

487
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:27,370
The height of a parallelogram
is a circle's radius?

488
00:30:28,200 --> 00:30:31,200
Ryota, isn't Math fun?

489
00:30:31,200 --> 00:30:32,300
Yeah!

490
00:30:32,300 --> 00:30:34,920
- That's so interesting!
- No way!

491
00:30:43,950 --> 00:30:45,830
["Please"]

492
00:30:45,830 --> 00:30:47,070
Good work.

493
00:30:47,070 --> 00:30:48,970
Likewise.

494
00:30:51,030 --> 00:30:52,600
Ms. Nashiki.

495
00:30:52,600 --> 00:30:54,720
Mr. Majima?

496
00:30:54,720 --> 00:30:58,320
As a fellow third grade teacher,
I raise issue with your methods!

497
00:30:59,510 --> 00:31:05,020
You haven't used tablets in your class once
since we returned from break.

498
00:31:05,600 --> 00:31:07,710
- Right...
- Ms. Nashiki.

499
00:31:07,710 --> 00:31:09,480
What are you doing?

500
00:31:09,480 --> 00:31:13,810
Do you plan to ignore the Ministry's
GIGA Schooling Curriculum?

501
00:31:13,810 --> 00:31:16,140
This curriculum is mandated by the nation.

502
00:31:16,140 --> 00:31:20,530
They've invested hundreds of millions of yen
to teach our kids about ICT.

503
00:31:21,090 --> 00:31:25,220
Taxpaying money is used to nurture
the future of these children.

504
00:31:25,220 --> 00:31:27,200
But you...

505
00:31:28,100 --> 00:31:30,000
Because of your methods...

506
00:31:32,580 --> 00:31:34,660
Sano, what are you doing?

507
00:31:36,100 --> 00:31:37,330
Hey!

508
00:31:37,850 --> 00:31:41,150
- Are you okay?
- Are you okay?

509
00:31:42,150 --> 00:31:44,680
Knock it off, Sano.

510
00:31:44,680 --> 00:31:46,990
You're causing trouble for your classmates!

511
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:50,360
Then kick me out!

512
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:53,600
Mr. Majima, I'll leave Class 1.

513
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:56,190
I wanna go to Class 3 instead!

514
00:31:56,190 --> 00:31:57,270
Class 3?

515
00:31:57,270 --> 00:31:59,880
Please, let me go to Class 3.

516
00:31:59,880 --> 00:32:02,710
I wanna join Ms. Nashiki's class!

517
00:32:05,180 --> 00:32:08,660
We are following proper guidance
when giving our lessons.

518
00:32:08,660 --> 00:32:12,380
If you ignore the rules,
it will cause trouble in the other classes,

519
00:32:12,380 --> 00:32:14,340
do you understand that?

520
00:32:17,240 --> 00:32:21,520
He makes a point.
Class 3 has been quite loud recently.

521
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:24,770
You use the library,
and have them move desks during lessons.

522
00:32:24,770 --> 00:32:27,970
But they're learning!

523
00:32:27,970 --> 00:32:29,810
Ms. Nashiki.

524
00:32:30,640 --> 00:32:34,320
You are hindering the future of these youth.

525
00:32:34,320 --> 00:32:38,810
If you continue with your ways,
your kids will become the underdogs.

526
00:32:39,500 --> 00:32:41,370
Underdogs?

527
00:32:43,050 --> 00:32:45,660
All my students have learned.

528
00:32:45,660 --> 00:32:48,430
Today, we studied the essence of a circle.

529
00:32:48,430 --> 00:32:52,830
Essence? Just have them
look it up on their tablets.

530
00:32:52,830 --> 00:32:56,530
Videos, apps,
many things can teach them that.

531
00:32:56,530 --> 00:32:59,370
You must use their time efficiently.

532
00:32:59,370 --> 00:33:00,930
Exactly.

533
00:33:00,930 --> 00:33:03,020
You are disregarding
your responsibility as a teacher!

534
00:33:03,020 --> 00:33:04,550
Mr. Majima, Ms. Nashiki...

535
00:33:04,550 --> 00:33:07,010
Disregarding my responsibility?

536
00:33:08,300 --> 00:33:10,980
I don't get a word you're saying.

537
00:33:10,980 --> 00:33:13,050
- Pardon?
- I mean,

538
00:33:13,050 --> 00:33:16,740
my students discovered for themselves
what makes a circle.

539
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:19,050
They won't forget this experience for life.

540
00:33:19,050 --> 00:33:24,680
But criticizing my methods,
because I won't use these "tools"?

541
00:33:24,680 --> 00:33:27,170
That's not what teaching is about.

542
00:33:27,170 --> 00:33:30,100
Studying and the use of tools
are separate lessons to learn.

543
00:33:30,100 --> 00:33:34,530
Is it really our jobs to teach them
how to use these tools?

544
00:33:34,530 --> 00:33:38,040
These children are born as digital natives.

545
00:33:38,040 --> 00:33:40,330
Tablets will continue to be
a part of their lives.

546
00:33:40,330 --> 00:33:43,250
They have to know how to use this technology!

547
00:33:43,250 --> 00:33:47,280
But is it really our job as teachers
to teach them that?

548
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:52,730
Aren't teachers supposed to teach kids
how to think through problems?

549
00:33:55,850 --> 00:33:58,490
Their time is limited, right?

550
00:33:58,490 --> 00:34:03,020
Then we ought to teach them how to study.

551
00:34:03,900 --> 00:34:07,180
Even so, this is something
we have to teach them.

552
00:34:09,170 --> 00:34:11,350
Tablets are merely tools.

553
00:34:11,350 --> 00:34:13,240
You're right about that.

554
00:34:13,240 --> 00:34:15,960
But knowing how to use tech is the norm now.

555
00:34:15,960 --> 00:34:19,300
And if you can't, then you're looked down on.

556
00:34:19,300 --> 00:34:21,500
That's the reality we live in.

557
00:34:22,040 --> 00:34:26,230
But these tools are very expensive.

558
00:34:26,230 --> 00:34:30,200
Tablets, game consoles -
not every household can afford them.

559
00:34:30,200 --> 00:34:34,950
So these opportunities at school
may be their only chance to acclimate.

560
00:34:36,930 --> 00:34:43,000
By utilizing these tools,
kids may even discover new talents.

561
00:34:43,910 --> 00:34:48,240
It's not only textbook knowledge
we teach, right?

562
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:53,860
Wow...

563
00:34:53,860 --> 00:34:56,740
Everyone's already adapted to using 3.14.

564
00:34:56,740 --> 00:35:02,680
Indeed. The number I discovered
has become but a tool to my pupils.

565
00:35:04,010 --> 00:35:06,210
Master Seki...

566
00:35:06,210 --> 00:35:08,160
How can you smile about such a thing?

567
00:35:08,160 --> 00:35:10,410
If it were me, I would feel vexed.

568
00:35:10,410 --> 00:35:11,380
Oh?

569
00:35:11,380 --> 00:35:14,460
I mean, you worked so hard
to reach the number 3.14...

570
00:35:14,460 --> 00:35:15,880
Quite stingy, aren't you?

571
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:17,090
Oh, shush.

572
00:35:17,090 --> 00:35:21,250
It doesn't really matter
who discovered the number.

573
00:35:21,250 --> 00:35:26,210
For my goals lie much higher than this.

574
00:35:31,510 --> 00:35:33,300
The circumference ratio.

575
00:35:33,300 --> 00:35:35,910
Only this number will be passed on,

576
00:35:35,910 --> 00:35:38,420
like a handy tool to utilize.

577
00:35:38,420 --> 00:35:40,570
What do you think happens next?

578
00:35:41,310 --> 00:35:47,150
This number will continue supporting people
for generations to come.

579
00:35:48,580 --> 00:35:54,600
Even if our names fade into obscurity.

580
00:35:56,720 --> 00:36:01,170
It shall become a tool anyone can use.

581
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:06,030
A tool, passed into the future.

582
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:14,820
A tool used right

583
00:36:16,360 --> 00:36:19,800
can help a child learn.

584
00:36:25,670 --> 00:36:28,480
Mr. Majima, Ms. Akutsu.

585
00:36:29,110 --> 00:36:31,140
I'm so sorry.

586
00:36:31,140 --> 00:36:33,530
My thinking was shallow and foolish.

587
00:36:33,530 --> 00:36:35,410
Ms. Nashiki?

588
00:36:36,320 --> 00:36:41,160
You two both spoke of lessons
necessary for this era.

589
00:36:41,160 --> 00:36:42,230
No, I...

590
00:36:42,230 --> 00:36:46,330
I am truly sorry to you both.

591
00:36:47,150 --> 00:36:51,620
Goodness, I can't with this...

592
00:36:53,310 --> 00:36:55,120
What's going on?

593
00:36:55,120 --> 00:36:59,300
- Has there been a fight?
- No, ma'am.

594
00:36:59,300 --> 00:37:03,140
Mr. Majima, did you make Ms. Nashiki cry?

595
00:37:03,140 --> 00:37:04,590
No, I...

596
00:37:04,590 --> 00:37:06,640
Ms. Principal, this was because...

597
00:37:06,640 --> 00:37:11,850
I don't know what happened,
but making her apologize here?

598
00:37:11,850 --> 00:37:14,580
I'd consider that workplace harassment.

599
00:37:14,580 --> 00:37:17,210
No, erm...

600
00:37:17,210 --> 00:37:22,550
I am not giving you a warning.
Just some friendly advice.

601
00:37:22,550 --> 00:37:24,600
Ms. Principal, you misunderstand.

602
00:37:24,600 --> 00:37:28,920
These two were only teaching me
something important.

603
00:37:28,920 --> 00:37:32,460
I was apologizing for my ignorance.

604
00:37:32,460 --> 00:37:34,820
I'm sorry you all had to watch.

605
00:37:34,820 --> 00:37:36,830
And I apologize for the ruckus.

606
00:37:37,640 --> 00:37:39,240
I see.

607
00:37:39,240 --> 00:37:42,020
- Then nothing happened?
- Yes, ma'am.

608
00:37:43,710 --> 00:37:46,230
Then I'll let this slide.

609
00:37:47,660 --> 00:37:49,970
Talk to me if you need anything.

610
00:37:49,970 --> 00:37:53,150
So, what should we tell the Ministry?

611
00:37:53,150 --> 00:37:54,600
Right, the Ministry.

612
00:37:54,600 --> 00:37:56,050
Sorry.

613
00:37:56,620 --> 00:37:59,340
Alright, let's leave it at that.

614
00:37:59,340 --> 00:38:01,380
Everyone back to work.

615
00:38:43,500 --> 00:38:48,200
<i>Even if our names fade into obscurity.</i>

616
00:38:49,020 --> 00:38:52,470
<i>It will become a tool,
passed into the future.</i>

617
00:38:54,020 --> 00:38:56,010
Master Seki...

618
00:38:57,150 --> 00:38:59,740
You're amazing, Master Seki.

619
00:38:59,740 --> 00:39:03,020
You always look far towards the future.

620
00:39:03,020 --> 00:39:06,570
Yet you seem to forget every meal.

621
00:39:07,920 --> 00:39:11,170
Only you scold me, Iroha.

622
00:39:11,170 --> 00:39:13,190
Isn't that right, Ritsu?

623
00:39:19,540 --> 00:39:22,110
Why couldn't I be a wrestler?

624
00:39:22,930 --> 00:39:24,670
What do you mean?

625
00:39:24,670 --> 00:39:29,300
If not a wrestler, then a master swordsman.

626
00:39:29,300 --> 00:39:31,680
Or even a ninja.

627
00:39:32,280 --> 00:39:35,810
Then I could have protected you.

628
00:39:38,620 --> 00:39:41,120
While you take on an important job,

629
00:39:41,120 --> 00:39:43,980
I would always protect you at your side,

630
00:39:43,980 --> 00:39:46,800
whether it rains droplets or spears.

631
00:39:46,800 --> 00:39:48,180
Isn't that right?

632
00:39:49,990 --> 00:39:52,050
You already protect me plenty.

633
00:39:52,050 --> 00:39:54,350
Is that good?

634
00:39:54,350 --> 00:39:55,820
You're a speedy eater.

635
00:39:58,460 --> 00:40:02,380
I have sometimes hoped
you were instead born to a warrior family.

636
00:40:03,840 --> 00:40:08,480
After all, I have a duty
of continuing my family lineage.

637
00:40:11,210 --> 00:40:14,740
What do you...

638
00:40:14,740 --> 00:40:18,820
Mathematics is my priority.

639
00:40:19,570 --> 00:40:22,830
As such, I offer myself wholly
to the god of mathematics.

640
00:40:24,180 --> 00:40:26,840
I believe whoever I am to wed

641
00:40:28,050 --> 00:40:30,100
will feel quite lonesome as a result.

642
00:40:30,820 --> 00:40:32,620
No.

643
00:40:33,840 --> 00:40:35,570
If it were me, I would never...

644
00:40:35,570 --> 00:40:38,080
But you are the daughter of a commoner.

645
00:40:39,840 --> 00:40:43,760
An eccentric one,
who has a passion for mathematics.

646
00:40:45,120 --> 00:40:49,440
That fact itself made me overjoyed.

647
00:40:50,790 --> 00:40:54,620
For it meant we could be together forever.

648
00:40:56,380 --> 00:41:00,010
No one can predict
the events that are to come.

649
00:41:01,070 --> 00:41:04,590
And as a warrior, I must obey my orders.

650
00:41:06,360 --> 00:41:08,000
However...

651
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:14,120
Iroha, thanks to mathematics,
we are connected,

652
00:41:14,120 --> 00:41:16,520
no matter how far apart we are.

653
00:41:18,790 --> 00:41:22,360
Yes. I understand that well.

654
00:41:23,910 --> 00:41:27,230
I am blessed to have met you, Master Seki.

655
00:41:34,250 --> 00:41:35,670
Iroha.

656
00:41:41,590 --> 00:41:44,840
I have a gift for you
as thanks for these rice balls.

657
00:41:45,810 --> 00:41:50,210
While theorizing the circumference ratio,
I came up with a certain question.

658
00:41:50,210 --> 00:41:53,480
A new question of yours, Master Seki?

659
00:41:55,570 --> 00:41:57,750
I have been summoned

660
00:41:59,460 --> 00:42:01,380
by the leader of Kofu.

661
00:42:07,140 --> 00:42:12,020
The day I leave, I will post my gift to you
at the Hachiman shrine.

662
00:42:13,160 --> 00:42:17,570
It is because I believe in you,
that I will hold nothing back.

663
00:42:18,270 --> 00:42:19,850
<i>I will leave the answer a blank.</i>

664
00:42:19,850 --> 00:42:21,470
[Hachiman Shrine]

665
00:42:21,470 --> 00:42:24,180
<i>Compete with mathematicians</i>

666
00:42:25,360 --> 00:42:27,010
from all over Japan.

667
00:42:30,510 --> 00:42:31,780
Yes, Master Seki.

668
00:42:33,800 --> 00:42:35,660
I will solve your equation.

669
00:42:35,660 --> 00:42:39,140
I will answer whatever question you give me.

670
00:42:39,140 --> 00:42:40,860
It is a promise.

671
00:42:42,140 --> 00:42:46,550
Mathematics will always tie us together.

672
00:42:53,340 --> 00:42:55,600
No matter how far apart we are.

673
00:43:31,870 --> 00:43:33,820
Ms. Nashiki?

674
00:43:42,860 --> 00:43:46,070
Ms. Nashiki! Step back!

675
00:43:46,070 --> 00:43:48,940
- Ikuru?
- Step back!

676
00:43:58,650 --> 00:44:00,700
No...

677
00:44:01,820 --> 00:44:03,820
How?

678
00:44:05,130 --> 00:44:07,390
How could I have forgotten?

679
00:44:07,390 --> 00:44:10,350
I made such an important promise...

680
00:44:10,940 --> 00:44:13,650
Promise? What promise?

681
00:44:13,650 --> 00:44:16,180
Ikuru, sorry. I have to go.

682
00:44:16,180 --> 00:44:18,600
Go where?

683
00:44:18,600 --> 00:44:20,400
Wait!

684
00:44:35,040 --> 00:44:37,410
Why would you try to go alone?

685
00:44:39,740 --> 00:44:43,410
Ms. Nashiki, you know nothing
about this modern era.

686
00:44:46,130 --> 00:44:50,260
Why do you think I'm here for you?

687
00:44:54,610 --> 00:44:57,220
[Afterschool Club]

688
00:44:59,070 --> 00:45:00,810
See you!

689
00:45:14,810 --> 00:45:16,240
Naoya.

690
00:45:16,960 --> 00:45:18,710
Dad!

691
00:45:20,690 --> 00:45:23,110
- School's finished?
- Yup.

692
00:45:24,440 --> 00:45:28,140
Dad's still got work,
so get yourself some dinner, alright?

693
00:45:29,030 --> 00:45:31,130
- Dad.
- Yeah?

694
00:45:31,130 --> 00:45:33,740
I'm not gonna go to school anymore.

695
00:45:34,640 --> 00:45:37,360
Why not? Are they bullying you again?

696
00:45:37,360 --> 00:45:41,200
No. I don't wanna be in Class 1 anymore.

697
00:45:41,200 --> 00:45:43,750
I wanna join Class 3.

698
00:45:43,750 --> 00:45:45,530
Class 3?

699
00:45:45,530 --> 00:45:49,930
Yeah. Class 3's teacher is super cool!

700
00:45:49,930 --> 00:45:52,400
Her name is Ms. Nashiki!

701
00:46:00,760 --> 00:46:04,340
If she's so great,
Dad would love to meet her.

702
00:46:04,340 --> 00:46:05,710
Yeah!
